From: venera_d3d12
Subject: venus: poll the first round of relax sleeps by yielding on Windows

vn_relax polls fences, semaphores, queries and ring progress with sleeps
starting at 160 us: 16 of those (~2.5 ms), then 16 of 320 us, 32 of 640 us
and so on. Windows cannot sleep for less than ~0.5 ms even on a
high-resolution timer (a 160 us wait takes ~530 us), so the first round polls
at a third of the intended rate, and that is where most GPU waits end: the
host GPU had finished a median ~480 us before the guest noticed (Steel Nomad
Light, correlated host and guest traces). Yield until each of the first
round's deadlines instead, which polls on time; later rounds sleep as before,
so a long wait (idle, vsync) spins for at most ~2.5 ms.

Steel Nomad Light with 0008: ~6000 -> 6517, host GPU 72% -> 77% busy, guest
vCPU ~2.2 -> ~3.4 cores during the run. Spinning in every round as well gave
6599 at ~3.5 cores, for waits of up to ~50 ms.

--- a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_common.c	2026-09-27 03:39:26.000000000 +0800
+++ b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_common.c	2026-11-06 22:59:29.226198311 +0800
@@ -291,6 +291,20 @@
    }

    const uint32_t shift = util_last_bit(*iter) - busy_wait_order - 1;
+#ifdef _WIN32
+   /* Windows cannot sleep for less than ~0.5 ms, so the first round of
+    * sleeps would poll at a third of the intended rate, and most GPU waits
+    * end within it. Yield until each of those deadlines instead; later,
+    * longer sleeps (from ~2.5 ms into the wait) use the timer.
+    */
+   if (shift == 0) {
+      const int64_t deadline =
+         os_time_get_nano() + base_sleep_us * INT64_C(1000);
+      while (os_time_get_nano() < deadline)
+         thrd_yield();
+      return;
+   }
+#endif
    os_time_sleep(base_sleep_us << shift);
 }
